---
onetaskgraph_template: 1
description: >-
  A plan's budgets document: the realistic workload, the checklist of concerns each given a
  budget or a reason it needs none, the answer to the 10x question, and the budgets the plan
  proposes. The planner writes it as the project document `<project>-budgets`; `just
  review-plan`, `just check-plan` and the design-document writer read its answers. The
  budgets file and its result format are onebudgetspec's; `docs/budgets.md` says which of
  its keys these answers name.
variables:
  workload:
    description: >-
      The realistic usage the plan's budgets hold at, with numbers: data volume and shape,
      concurrency, items per run.
    type: text
    required: true
  checklist:
    description: >-
      One object per concern the plan was checked against, with three string keys:
      `concern` (the concern, such as latency or gate time), `budget` (the `id` of the entry
      of `budgets` that covers it) and `not_applicable` (a one-line "n/a because ..."). Exactly
      one of `budget` and `not_applicable` is non-empty; the other is `""`.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
  ten_x:
    description: >-
      The answer to "at 10x realistic usage, what does the product owner notice getting worse
      first?", naming the `id` of the budget that covers it or saying why none does.
    type: text
    required: true
  # llmlint: ignore-block[contracts_have_one_source_or_a_drift_gate] The `budgets` description: this restates the onebudgetspec keys a plan's budgets document names, which the task that added it requires; their reconciliation with the installed library is the `aio-budgets` node's, which adopts that library here, and the vocabularies in it are held to `orchestrator/plan_budgets.py` by `tests/test_plan_budgets_template.py` and `tests/test_plan_budgets.py`.
  budgets:
    description: >-
      One object per budget the plan proposes, or `[]` when it proposes none, with these
      keys. `id` (string, the budget's id in its budgets file). `repository` (string, the
      normalized origin `host/owner/name` of the repository the budget goes in). `file`
      (string, the repository-relative path of the `budgets.yaml` it goes in: `budgets.yaml`
      for a repo-wide budget, or a project's own file for a scoped one). `measure` (string,
      what is measured, in words, as near as possible to where the product owner feels it).
      `inner_measure_reason` (string, why the measure is taken further inward than that, or
      `""` when it is not). `unit` (string), `direction` (string, `max` or `min`) and
      `threshold` (a number), each meaning what it means in onebudgetspec. `workload`
      (string, the realistic workload the threshold holds at, which is never written into
      the budgets file). `evidence` (string, where the number came from: a spike report's id
      and figures, or the measurement it rests on). `command` (string, the command that
      performs the measurement, as it will be registered). `node` (string, the id of the plan
      node that owns the budget's command). `file_change` (string, `add`, `change` or
      `none`).
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
  # llmlint: ignore-end[contracts_have_one_source_or_a_drift_gate]
  repo_wide_effects:
    description: >-
      At least one object per repository the plan's tasks change, with three string keys:
      `repository` (its normalized origin), `budget` (the `id` of a budget in that
      repository's root `budgets.yaml`, or `""` when it has none) and `effect` (the expected
      effect of the plan on it, never empty, and `none` when there is none).
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
  realistic_data:
    description: >-
      One object per kind of data the plan makes realistic, or `[]`, with four string keys:
      `data` (what the data is), `choice` (`generator`, `fixture`, `hybrid` or
      `real-sample`), `reason` (why, weighed on realism, generation cost, fixture cost and
      upkeep) and `artifact` (the repository artifact spikes, checks and later plans share).
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
  spike_findings:
    description: >-
      One object per spike finding that changed the plan, or `[]`, with three string keys:
      `spike` (the spike's id), `finding` (what it found) and `changed` (what in the plan it
      changed).
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
---
{#- A table cell is one line holding no bare column delimiter, whatever an answer says. -#}
{% macro cell(value) %}{{ value | string | replace("|", "\\|") | replace("\r\n", " ") | replace("\n", " ") | replace("\r", " ") }}{% endmacro %}
{% block document_guidance %}
{#
  THE READERS. The plan review, the plan check and the design-document writer read this
  document's answers; a person reads the design document it feeds. Every section below
  renders from one answer, and the closing Record section carries all of them as one line
  of JSON, which is what the plan check and the review read, wherever the document has been
  copied to.

  THIS TEMPLATE. Guidance is always a Jinja comment, never an HTML comment, so a rendered
  document carries none of it. The front matter states the data shape of every answer;
  `docs/budgets.md` states the principles the answers are held to.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block workload %}
## Workload

{{ workload }}

{% endblock %}
{% block checklist %}
## Checklist

| Concern | Budget | Not applicable because |
| --- | --- | --- |
{% for entry in checklist %}
| {{ cell(entry.concern) }} | {{ cell(entry.budget) }} | {{ cell(entry.not_applicable) }} |
{% endfor %}

{% endblock %}
{% block ten_x %}
## At 10x realistic usage

{{ ten_x }}

{% endblock %}
{% block budgets %}
## Budgets

{% for budget in budgets %}
### `{{ budget.id }}`

- **Registered in:** `{{ budget.file }}` of `{{ budget.repository }}` ({{ budget.file_change }})
- **Measure:** {{ budget.measure }}
{% if budget.inner_measure_reason %}
- **Inner measure, because:** {{ budget.inner_measure_reason }}
{% endif %}
- **Target:** {{ budget.direction }} {{ budget.threshold }} {{ budget.unit }}
- **Workload:** {{ budget.workload }}
- **Evidence:** {{ budget.evidence }}
- **Command:** `{{ budget.command }}`
- **Owned by node:** `{{ budget.node }}`

{% else %}
This plan proposes no budget.

{% endfor %}
{% endblock %}
{% block repo_wide_effects %}
## Repo-wide effects

| Repository | Repo-wide budget | Expected effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
{% for entry in repo_wide_effects %}
| {{ cell(entry.repository) }} | {{ cell(entry.budget or "none") }} | {{ cell(entry.effect) }} |
{% endfor %}

{% endblock %}
{% block realistic_data %}
## Realistic data

{% for entry in realistic_data %}
- **{{ entry.data }}** — {{ entry.choice }}: {{ entry.reason }} (`{{ entry.artifact }}`)
{% else %}
This plan makes no data realistic.
{% endfor %}

{% endblock %}
{% block spike_findings %}
## Spike findings

{% for entry in spike_findings %}
- **{{ entry.spike }}** — {{ entry.finding }} Changed: {{ entry.changed }}
{% else %}
No spike finding changed this plan.
{% endfor %}

{% endblock %}
{#- The record is the last thing in the document and one line long, which is how a reader finds it. -#}
## Record

```json
{{ {"workload": workload, "checklist": checklist, "ten_x": ten_x, "budgets": budgets, "repo_wide_effects": repo_wide_effects, "realistic_data": realistic_data, "spike_findings": spike_findings} | tojson }}
```
